.TH FERAISEEXCEPT 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
feraiseexcept \- raise floating-point exception flags
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.PP
.BI "int feraiseexcept(int " excepts );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR feraiseexcept ()
function raises the floating-point exception flags specified by the bitwise-OR
expression
.IR excepts ,
as if the corresponding exceptional floating-point operations had been
performed.
Unlike
.BR fesetexceptflag (3),
which only sets the sticky bits,
.BR feraiseexcept ()
may trigger a floating-point trap (SIGFPE) if the corresponding exception is
unmasked.
.PP
The argument
.I excepts
is a bitwise OR of zero or more of the following macros:
.TP
.B FE_INVALID
Raise the invalid-operation exception (for example, 0/0 or
.BR sqrt (\-1)).
.TP
.B FE_DIVBYZERO
Raise the division-by-zero exception.
.TP
.B FE_OVERFLOW
Raise the overflow exception.
In accordance with C99 \(sc7.6.2.3, raising
.B FE_OVERFLOW
also raises
.BR FE_INEXACT .
.TP
.B FE_UNDERFLOW
Raise the underflow exception.
In accordance with C99 \(sc7.6.2.3, raising
.B FE_UNDERFLOW
also raises
.BR FE_INEXACT .
.TP
.B FE_INEXACT
Raise the inexact-result exception.
.TP
.B FE_ALL_EXCEPT
Raise all exception flags.
.PP
When both
.B FE_INVALID
and
.B FE_DIVBYZERO
are specified together, the implementation may raise them in any order.
.PP
Passing zero for
.I excepts
is valid and is a no-op.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR feraiseexcept ()
returns zero on success.
A non-zero return value indicates that the implementation was unable to raise
one or more of the requested exceptions.
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined.
The function does not set
.IR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example raises
.B FE_INVALID
and
.BR FE_OVERFLOW ,
then tests the resulting flags.
Note that raising
.B FE_OVERFLOW
also sets
.BR FE_INEXACT .
.PP
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
#include <fenv.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    feclearexcept(FE_ALL_EXCEPT);

    feraiseexcept(FE_INVALID);
    if (fetestexcept(FE_INVALID))
        printf("FE_INVALID is set.\\n");

    feclearexcept(FE_ALL_EXCEPT);

    /* Raising FE_OVERFLOW implicitly also sets FE_INEXACT (C99 s7.6.2.3). */
    feraiseexcept(FE_OVERFLOW);
    if (fetestexcept(FE_OVERFLOW))
        printf("FE_OVERFLOW is set.\\n");
    if (fetestexcept(FE_INEXACT))
        printf("FE_INEXACT is implicitly set by FE_OVERFLOW.\\n");

    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fenv (7),
.BR feclearexcept (3),
.BR fetestexcept (3),
.BR fesetexceptflag (3)
